st, the relevant/related context files (retrieved as ground-truth relevant files) are placed before the target file being completed. In cross_file_random, context files are sampled randomly. Actually let me recall precisely:\n\nFrom the RepoBench paper: RepoBench-C constructs the completion prompt as: \"related files + target file with preceding code\" and asks the model to complete the next line(s). The \"cross_file_first\" setting places the related files (files that are known to be relevant, e.g., files that the target file imports/references) in the context BEFORE the target file. The \"cross_file_random\" setting places randomly sampled files. So cross_file_first = oracle-ish relevant context ordering.\n\nActually, more precisely from the repo (leogao2/repobench): The task is \"complete the next line\" given preceding code in the same file plus up to N related files placed in context. The exact details:\n- RepoBench-C-v1.1.0-python dataset on HuggingFace.\n- Each sample has \"context_files\" (dict of file_name -> content), \"target_file\" (the file being completed), and \"golden_line\"/\"next_line\" (the ground truth next line), plus \"golden_functions\" maybe.\n- Setting: cross_file_first means the context files are the files that appear BEFORE the target in the file list (i.e., files that precede the target file in the repository's file ordering / appear earlier). Hmm.\n\nLet me recall the actual RepoBench-C construction. From the paper:\n\n\"RepoBench-C: Completion\" — For each target, they retrieve the most relevant files (using BM25 or the ground-truth related files) and concatenate them as context. Two settings:\n- Cross-file First: put the retrieved related files in context before the target file's preceding content.\n- Cross-file Random: randomly choose files.\n\nIn the released dataset (RepoBench-C), each test sample already includes \"context_files\" — I believe these are exactly the files used. The \"cross_file_first\" setting is one of the two modes in which context was assembled. The HF dataset has splits with context files already assembled per setting? Actually I think the dataset is the same and the setting determines construction at eval time from repo snapshots.\n\nGiven the actual repo (leogao2/repobench), the evaluation script constructs prompts. The dataset has a \"prompt\" field already assembled in some cases.\n\nI need to be careful: I cannot verify details without network. So I will mark exact field names / dataset revision as assumptions and cite the public paper/repo as the source of truth that the experimenter must pin down during implementation.\n\nLet me structure the plan:\n\n### Approach\nDesign intent: a controlled, single-variable comparison. Keep the prompt template and decoding fixed; vary only the model. Report Pass@k-style next-line match rate and syntax validity (python ast.parse), plus execution-based checks where feasible (run `python -m py_compile` or actually execute candidate line with the preceding context in a sandbox — but executing arbitrary code is risky; use ast.parse for syntax validity and optional isolated execution in a subprocess with timeout for behavioral correctness).\n\n### Models\n- Small: GPT-2 (124M \"gpt2\") — the canonical small LM. Optionally gpt2-xl (1.5B) to show within-family scaling. Both via HuggingFace transformers AutoModelForCausalLM / GPT2LMHeadModel.\n- Large code model: CodeLlama-7b (meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf or CodeLlama-7b-Instruct? The base/hf is the code completion model; for completion use the base CodeLlama-7b-hf, which is 7B parameters, context 16k, byte-level BPE tokenizer). Instantiate via transformers AutoModelForCausalLM with dtype float16/bf16, load in 8-bit/4-bit if needed — but no GPU, so it's CPU-only. On CPU, 7B float16 inference is slow but feasible for a bounded sample set given 224 cores and ~2TB RAM. Actually wait: it's design-only, so compute planning is for the \"if it were run\" scenario. But I should still plan a realistic design the experimenter could execute: recommend running CodeLlama-7b on CPU with a capped sample subset (e.g., 200–500 samples × 5–10 candidates) and quantized/int8 to fit; estimate throughput.\n\nEstimating CodeLlama-7b on 224-core CPU: FP32 7B inference ~ maybe 5-20 tokens/sec with a large core count... 7B params in fp32 ≈ 28GB weights; RAM is ~2TB so fine. Generation of 128 tokens × 10 candidates × 500 samples = 640K tokens; at ~10 tok/s per forward that's 640K/10 = 64,000 sec ≈ 18 hrs per run — borderline. Could reduce candidates or sample count. I'll present a compute budget plan with a fallback subset rule.\n\n### Tokenizer/VLA\n- GPT-2: Byte-Pair Encoding, vocab 50257, max ctx 1024 tokens.\n- CodeLlama: SentencePiece byte-level BPE, vocab 32016, max ctx 16384 (16k).\n- Mismatch: GPT-2's 1024-token context vs CodeLlama's 16k. To make comparison fair, truncate context to a fixed token budget that fits GPT-2's window — e.g., keep the last N tokens of the assembled prompt where N = 1024 − reserved generation space. Standardize N = 768 prompt tokens (leave 256 for completion) for BOTH models. This removes the \"CodeLlama can see more context\" confound. Alternative: run CodeLlama also at its full window as a secondary (non-primary) result to show effect of context length. But primary comparison should hold context fixed.\n\nActually an important design decision: should we hold context length fixed (fair comparison, GPT-2-limited) or let each model use its full window (realistic)? For \"how does performance compare\" the cleanest answer isolates model capability, so fix the token budget at GPT-2's window (or at a shared budget). I'll propose primary = shared 1024-token cap; secondary = CodeLlama full 16k to quantify the context-capacity effect. Mark as a design choice.\n\n### Cross-file context construction\nRepoBench-C sample: contains the repo, target file, its preceding source code, and context files (ground truth relevant files). For cross_file_first: order = [context files relevant to the target, in the order they appear in the repo file listing / as given in the dataset] then the target file's prefix (code preceding the target line). Token budget: fit as many context files as possible after the target prefix within the shared budget; if overflow, truncate from the oldest/farthest context files (keep target-file prefix always — it's the most informative). Deterministic truncation rule: always keep target prefix; then add context files in given order from the most related; if exceeding budget, drop the least-related tail of context files (or truncate context file contents at file boundaries to keep whole top-K files).\n\nPrecise prompt template:\n```\n<context files, sequentially>\n...target file prefix (all code before the target line)...\n<insertion point for next line>\n```\nThe model completes the next line N tokens/one line. Since RepoBench-C's target is \"the next line(s)\", evaluation generates and we compare.\n\n### Decoding config\nFix: greedy (temperature=0) for Pass@1 primary; and for Pass@k use nucleus sampling temperature=1.0, top_p=0.95, fixed seeds, k samples n=10 per instance (or 5), max_new_tokens = length of gold line tokens + some margin, or better: generate until the end-of-line / fixed cap of e.g. 64 tokens, then evaluate. Use same generation args for both models (temperature, top_p, top_k=None, repetition_penalty=1.0, do_sample for pass@k). Beam: greedy = beam_size 1; optionally beam_size=5 for a beam-variant Pass@1 but keep beam fixed across models if used. I'll fix: primary = greedy sampling (temp=0); secondary = nucleus sampling temp=1.0 top_p=0.95, n=10 candidates, seeds 0..9; max_new_tokens=60; pad/end tokens per model.\n\n### Metric\nPass@k definition: for each instance i with n generated candidates, pass@k = 1 if any candidate in the k selected passes, estimated via the unbiased estimator from Chen et al. (HumanEval): \npass@k = E[1 − C(n−c, k)/C(n, k)] where c = number of correct samples out of n. Report Pass@1, Pass@5 (with n=10). \"Pass\" = exact match of the gold next line after normalization (strip whitespace), OR token-f1, OR syntax-valid match. RepoBench-C's official metric is \"Next-line Completion Match\" — the exact-match of the completed next line (single-line). I recall RepoBench-C uses exact match on the next line as the metric (they compare the generated next line against the golden line). I'll report exact-match Pass@1/Pass@5 as primary, plus syntax validity rate (ast.parse over the target file prefix + candidate line) and execution-based pass rate as secondary feasibility where the gold line can be validated by executing in a sandboxed subprocess with timeout (risky, optional).\n\n### Compute/environment constraints\n- No GPU, no network, no datasest on disk, no ML packages, Python 3.14.4. So the design must include a \"setup\" step that assumes the real run happens in an environment with network access to install packages and download model weights + dataset. In the design, pin versions: transformers==4.44.x (or latest compatible with Python 3.10+ not 3.14 — note torch doesn't support 3.14 yet; plan for a Python 3.10/3.11 venv). Mark as assumption that implementation environment differs from this offline sandbox.\n\nGiven design-only scope, the deliverable design is what matters in a real environment with:\n- conda/venv Python 3.10/3.11, pip install torch, transformers, datasets, accelerate, sentencepiece, protobuf.\n- Model downloads: gpt2 (~500MB), CodeLlama-7b-hf (~13GB fp16).\n- Dataset: leogao2/repobench or RepoBench-C python split from HF.\n\n### Sample size / subsetting\nRepoBench-C python test set is large (I recall ~12.9K samples for completion? Actually RepoBench-C has 12,000+ completion examples across configs). Given LLM inference cost on CPU with 7B model, cap: random sample of N=400 instances (stratified by repo, fixed seed 42), or fewer like 200 with 5 candidates. Use a deterministic subsample with seed so results are reproducible. Report CI (bootstrap) around pass@1.\n\n### Assumptions/uncertainties to surface\n1. RepoBench dataset revision/version (v1.1.0?) and exact field names (\"context_files\", \"next_line\", \"golden_line\") — unverified, from public repo.\n2. Exact definition of cross_file_first (context file order and how many files) — from paper/repo, unverified locally.\n3. Model weights: gpt2 and meta CodeLlama-7b-hf require HF token/license acceptance — assume download available in run env (offline sandbox has none).\n4. Fairness of token-budget cap at 1024 — assumption/design choice.\n5. Pass@k estimator sample counts (n=10, k=1..5), seeds — assumption.\n6. Sample subset size and seed — assumption given budget.\n7. Next-line exact-match metric normalized (whitespace/leading) — assumption; alternative metrics optional.\n8. Tokenizers/VLA mismatch handled by capping shared context and per-model EOS handling.\n9. Confound: GPT-2 1024 ctx vs CodeLlama 16k — controlled by shared cap, plus secondary full-window run for CodeLlama.\n10. No torch support for Python 3.14 → need different Python version in real run env.\n11. Execution-based \"pass\" definition is confounded by environment; prefer syntax-validity + exact match as primary.\n\nNow present the plan via end_researcher_turn stage=plan.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 2) Proposed approach (high level)\n\n**Goal:** isolate model capability, holding prompt construction and decoding fixed. Compare on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` next-line completion.\n\n**Models**\n- Small LM: **GPT-2 (124M, `gpt2`)** as the primary \"small\" baseline (canonical small causal LM). Secondary: **gpt2-xl (1.5B)** to show within-family scaling. Instantiated via `transformers.GPT2LMHeadModel`.\n- Larger code model: **CodeLlama-7b** — instantiate the base code-completion variant (`meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`) via `AutoModelForCausalLM` in FP16 (≈13 GB weights), CPU inference (224 cores / ~2 TB RAM is enough to hold it in memory; no GPU). If throughput is a binding constraint, fall back to 8-bit (`bitsandbytes`) — marked as an assumption, not fixed.\n\n**Cross-file context construction (`cross_file_first`)** — based on the public RepoBench-C design: the relevant/ground-truth related files for the target are placed **before** the target file's own prefix. Concretely the assembled prompt is:\n`[context files in dataset order] + [target file prefix, i.e., all code before the target line] + <candidate continuation>`.\nTo keep the comparison fair (see confound below), truncate the *whole* prompt deterministically to a **shared token budget = 1024 tokens** (GPT-2's max window), leaving ~196 tokens for generation, and apply the identical rule to both models: **always keep the full target-file prefix first**, then fill the remaining budget with context files from most-relevant to least; on overflow drop the least-relevant/tail context files atomically at file boundaries (never mid-file).\n\n**Prompting a sample:** one prompt per RepoBench sample = concatenation above; the model is asked to continue at the insertion point. No special instruction text (base completion models; no chat template). Same template string for both models so only the tokenizer differs.\n\n**Decoding (fixed, single variable = model):**\n- Primary: **greedy** (`temperature=0`, `do_sample=False`) → Pass@1.\n- Secondary: **nucleus sampling**, `temperature=1.0`, `top_p=0.95`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `repetition_penalty=1.0`, n=10 candidates per instance, seeds 0–9 → Pass@k (Chen et al. unbiased estimator).\n- Same args object for both models; per-model EOS/pad token handling.\n\n**Metrics (precise definitions):**\n- **Next-line exact match Pass@k** (primary, matching RepoBench-C's \"next-line completion match\"): candidate is correct if it equals the golden next line after light normalization (strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse internal whitespace). `pass@k = 1 − C(n−c,k)/C(n,k)` with c=#correct of n=10 samples; report Pass@1, Pass@5 with bootstrap 95% CIs.\n- **Syntax-validity rate** (secondary): fraction of candidates where `ast.parse(target_prefix + candidate_line)` succeeds.\n- **Execution-based pass rate** (optional, risky): run each candidate line in a sandboxed subprocess (`timeout`, `-I` isolated interpreter, `subprocess` with resource limits) and compare stdout/exit; reported as exploratory only because it mixes in environment/externals.\n\n**Compute plan (for the real run environment, not here):** cap the subset so 7B CPU inference stays bounded — N=400 instances (seeded random subsample, stratified by repo), n=10 → ~800–4,000 generations total; with a fallback subset rule (N=200, n=5) if the 7B run exceeds a time budget (target < ~24 h on 224 cores). All subsetting by fixed seed 42 for reproducibility.\n\n## 3) Assumptions & uncertainties (all to be locked, none confirmed locally)\n- **A1 (dataset):** RepoBench-C Python split revision (e.g., v1.1.x) and exact field names (`context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`) as published (leogao2/repobench) — must be verified in the run environment.\n- **A2 (`cross_file_first` definition):** context files are the target's relevant files placed before the target prefix, in the dataset's documented order — taken from the paper/repo, unverifiable offline.\n- **A3 (weights/download):** `gpt2`/`gpt2-xl` and `CodeLlama-7b-hf` are downloadable in the run environment (HF token + meta license acceptance); the offline sandbox has neither network nor weights.\n- **A4 (environment mismatch):** the real run must use Python 3.10/3.11 in a venv (`torch` does not support 3.14), pinning `transformers`/`torch` versions — the sandbox's Python 3.14 with no packages is not the execution environment.\n- **A5 (fairness cap):** forcing both models to a shared 1024-token context is a deliberate design choice to remove the \"CodeLlama sees 16k, GPT-2 sees 1k\" confound; optionally a secondary CodeLlama run at full 16k window quantifies the context-capacity effect.\n- **A6 (Pass@k estimation):** n=10, seeds 0–9, unbiased estimator — an assumed convention, not a spec.\n- **A7 (sampling rule):** N=400 / fallback N=200 seeded subset — an assumption to satisfy time limits; results are a point estimate on a subset, with CIs.\n- **A8 (metric normalization):** exact-match definition and normalization (whitespace) is my choice; RepoBench's official exact-match may differ slightly.\n- **A9 (tokenizer/VLA mismatch):** GPT-2 BPE (50257, 1024 ctx) vs CodeLlama SentencePiece BPE (32016, 16k ctx) — handled by the shared-cap rule (A5) plus per-model EOS/pad defaults; token counts are model-specific so the shared \"1024 tokens\" differs in raw characters (minor residual noise, acknowledged).\n- **A10 (execution-based metric):** environment/import dependencies confound it; it is exploratory only.", "evidence": "Verified by inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; task.json's documents.track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Runtime: network disabled (pip download and DNS both fail), gpu_enabled false, cpu_affinity 224, ~2 TB RAM, datasets [] (no data on disk). Python 3.14.4 with importlib checks confirming torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy/safetensors/accelerate/HF hub all absent; no HF cache dirs. No RepoBench artifacts or official eval scripts anywhere on disk. Therefore the design relies on the public RepoBench paper/repo (Tian et al. 2023) and model cards for all dataset/setting/model details, which are flagged as assumptions.", "assumptions": ["A1: RepoBench-C Python dataset revision and exact field names from the public repo (not on disk).", "A2: cross_file_first = relevant files placed before the target prefix, in dataset-documented order (from paper).", "A3: gpt2/gpt2-xl and CodeLlama-7b-hf weights obtainable in the real run environment (offline sandbox has neither).", "A4: real run uses Python 3.10/3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers; sandbox Python 3.14 is not the execution environment.", "A5: shared 1024-token context cap is a chosen fairness control (CodeLlama's 16k vs GPT-2's 1k); optional full-window CodeLlama secondary run.", "A6: Pass@k uses n=10 samples, seeds 0-9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator.", "A7: seeded subset N=400 (fallback N=200, n=5) to bound 7B CPU inference time.", "A8: exact-match metric uses my whitespace/normalization rule; official match may differ.", "A9: tokenizer/VLA mismatch handled by shared cap + per-model EOS/pad; residual noise from differing vocab tokenization, acknowledged.", "A10: execution-based pass rate is exploratory only due to environment/import confounds."], "uncertainties": ["Exact RepoBench-C field names/prompt schema unverifiable offline (A1/A2).", "Whether CodeLlama-7b-hf CPU throughput allows N=400 within time budget (plan includes fallback subset rule).", "Whether any execution-based check is meaningful given no package/environment assumptions in the gold code (A10).", "Which official metric (exact next-line match) the RepoBench harness uses vs my normalization (A8)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-061e15895abe4a76878b5ee2c3534590", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-061e15895abe4a76878b5ee2c3534590", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"plan\", \"summary\": \"Present input inventory, proposed approach, and assumptions for RepoBench GPT-2 vs CodeLlama comparison\", \"answer\": \"## 2) Proposed approach (high level)\\n\\n**Goal:** isolate model capability, holding prompt construction and decoding fixed. Compare on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` next-line completion.\\n\\n**Models**\\n- Small LM: **GPT-2 (124M, `gpt2`)** as the primary \\\"small\\\" baseline (canonical small causal LM). Secondary: **gpt2-xl (1.5B)** to show within-family scaling. Instantiated via `transformers.GPT2LMHeadModel`.\\n- Larger code model: **CodeLlama-7b** \\u2014 instantiate the base code-completion variant (`meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`) via `AutoModelForCausalLM` in FP16 (\\u224813 GB weights), CPU inference (224 cores / ~2 TB RAM is enough to hold it in memory; no GPU). If throughput is a binding constraint, fall back to 8-bit (`bitsandbytes`) \\u2014 marked as an assumption, not fixed.\\n\\n**Cross-file context construction (`cross_file_first`)** \\u2014 based on the public RepoBench-C design: the relevant/ground-truth related files for the target are placed **before** the target file's own prefix. Concretely the assembled prompt is:\\n`[context files in dataset order] + [target file prefix, i.e., all code before the target line] + <candidate continuation>`.\\nTo keep the comparison fair (see confound below), truncate the *whole* prompt deterministically to a **shared token budget = 1024 tokens** (GPT-2's max window), leaving ~196 tokens for generation, and apply the identical rule to both models: **always keep the full target-file prefix first**, then fill the remaining budget with context files from most-relevant to least; on overflow drop the least-relevant/tail context files atomically at file boundaries (never mid-file).\\n\\n**Prompting a sample:** one prompt per RepoBench sample = concatenation above; the model is asked to continue at the insertion point. No special instruction text (base completion models; no chat template). Same template string for both models so only the tokenizer differs.\\n\\n**Decoding (fixed, single variable = model):**\\n- Primary: **greedy** (`temperature=0`, `do_sample=False`) \\u2192 Pass@1.\\n- Secondary: **nucleus sampling**, `temperature=1.0`, `top_p=0.95`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `repetition_penalty=1.0`, n=10 candidates per instance, seeds 0\\u20139 \\u2192 Pass@k (Chen et al. unbiased estimator).\\n- Same args object for both models; per-model EOS/pad token handling.\\n\\n**Metrics (precise definitions):**\\n- **Next-line exact match Pass@k** (primary, matching RepoBench-C's \\\"next-line completion match\\\"): candidate is correct if it equals the golden next line after light normalization (strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse internal whitespace). `pass@k = 1 \\u2212 C(n\\u2212c,k)/C(n,k)` with c=#correct of n=10 samples; report Pass@1, Pass@5 with bootstrap 95% CIs.\\n- **Syntax-validity rate** (secondary): fraction of candidates where `ast.parse(target_prefix + candidate_line)` succeeds.\\n- **Execution-based pass rate** (optional, risky): run each candidate line in a sandboxed subprocess (`timeout`, `-I` isolated interpreter, `subprocess` with resource limits) and compare stdout/exit; reported as exploratory only because it mixes in environment/externals.\\n\\n**Compute plan (for the real run environment, not here):** cap the subset so 7B CPU inference stays bounded \\u2014 N=400 instances (seeded random subsample, stratified by repo), n=10 \\u2192 ~800\\u20134,000 generations total; with a fallback subset rule (N=200, n=5) if the 7B run exceeds a time budget (target < ~24 h on 224 cores). All subsetting by fixed seed 42 for reproducibility.\\n\\n## 3) Assumptions & uncertainties (all to be locked, none confirmed locally)\\n- **A1 (dataset):** RepoBench-C Python split revision (e.g., v1.1.x) and exact field names (`context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`) as published (leogao2/repobench) \\u2014 must be verified in the run environment.\\n- **A2 (`cross_file_first` definition):** context files are the target's relevant files placed before the target prefix, in the dataset's documented order \\u2014 taken from the paper/repo, unverifiable offline.\\n- **A3 (weights/download):** `gpt2`/`gpt2-xl` and `CodeLlama-7b-hf` are downloadable in the run environment (HF token + meta license acceptance); the offline sandbox has neither network nor weights.\\n- **A4 (environment mismatch):** the real run must use Python 3.10/3.11 in a venv (`torch` does not support 3.14), pinning `transformers`/`torch` versions \\u2014 the sandbox's Python 3.14 with no packages is not the execution environment.\\n- **A5 (fairness cap):** forcing both models to a shared 1024-token context is a deliberate design choice to remove the \\\"CodeLlama sees 16k, GPT-2 sees 1k\\\" confound; optionally a secondary CodeLlama run at full 16k window quantifies the context-capacity effect.\\n- **A6 (Pass@k estimation):** n=10, seeds 0\\u20139, unbiased estimator \\u2014 an assumed convention, not a spec.\\n- **A7 (sampling rule):** N=400 / fallback N=200 seeded subset \\u2014 an assumption to satisfy time limits; results are a point estimate on a subset, with CIs.\\n- **A8 (metric normalization):** exact-match definition and normalization (whitespace) is my choice; RepoBench's official exact-match may differ slightly.\\n- **A9 (tokenizer/VLA mismatch):** GPT-2 BPE (50257, 1024 ctx) vs CodeLlama SentencePiece BPE (32016, 16k ctx) \\u2014 handled by the shared-cap rule (A5) plus per-model EOS/pad defaults; token counts are model-specific so the shared \\\"1024 tokens\\\" differs in raw characters (minor residual noise, acknowledged).\\n- **A10 (execution-based metric):** environment/import dependencies confound it; it is exploratory only.\", \"evidence\": \"Verified by inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; task.json's documents.track.md states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Runtime: network disabled (pip download and DNS both fail), gpu_enabled false, cpu_affinity 224, ~2 TB RAM, datasets [] (no data on disk). Python 3.14.4 with importlib checks confirming torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy/safetensors/accelerate/HF hub all absent; no HF cache dirs. No RepoBench artifacts or official eval scripts anywhere on disk. Therefore the design relies on the public RepoBench paper/repo (Tian et al. 2023) and model cards for all dataset/setting/model details, which are flagged as assumptions.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: RepoBench-C Python dataset revision and exact field names from the public repo (not on disk).\", \"A2: cross_file_first = relevant files placed before the target prefix, in dataset-documented order (from paper).\", \"A3: gpt2/gpt2-xl and CodeLlama-7b-hf weights obtainable in the real run environment (offline sandbox has neither).\", \"A4: real run uses Python 3.10/3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers; sandbox Python 3.14 is not the execution environment.\", \"A5: shared 1024-token context cap is a chosen fairness control (CodeLlama's 16k vs GPT-2's 1k); optional full-window CodeLlama secondary run.\", \"A6: Pass@k uses n=10 samples, seeds 0-9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator.\", \"A7: seeded subset N=400 (fallback N=200, n=5) to bound 7B CPU inference time.\", \"A8: exact-match metric uses my whitespace/normalization rule; official match may differ.\", \"A9: tokenizer/VLA mismatch handled by shared cap + per-model EOS/pad; residual noise from differing vocab tokenization, acknowledged.\", \"A10: execution-based pass rate is exploratory only due to environment/import confounds.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact RepoBench-C field names/prompt schema unverifiable offline (A1/A2).\", \"Whether CodeLlama-7b-hf CPU throughput allows N=400 within time budget (plan includes fallback subset rule).\", \"Whether any execution-based check is meaningful given no package/environment assumptions in the gold code (A10).\", \"Which official metric (exact next-line match) the RepoBench harness uses vs my normalization (A8).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-101956c2aa3b41709b8e3637631b627e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Present input inventory, proposed approach, and assumptions for RepoBench GPT-2 vs CodeLlama comparison", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
