{"id": "0eddad34-19d3-4803-9084-e74e2e2ee653", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:10:47.941082", "source": "user", "parent_id": "95538cc2-59cc-4906-9691-ae79e4b7f447", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The approach is well-reasoned and I approve it. Fold these refinements into the final design (not blockers):\\n\\n1) Keep the shared 1024-token context cap as the primary fairness control, and include the optional full-window (16k) CodeLlama run as a clearly labeled secondary analysis to quantify the context-capacity effect. Don't let it muddy the headline result.\\n\\n2) The execution-based \\\"pass rate\\\" on a single candidate line is confounded by imports, utility functions, and environment assumptions — mark it clearly exploratory, or drop it. The primary metrics should be next-line exact-match Pass@k and the syntax-validity rate.\\n\\n3) Pin the metric definitions precisely in the final design: the exact whitespace/normalization rule for exact match, the Pass@k estimator (n=10, seeds 0–9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator), bootstrap 95% CIs, and how empty/truncated candidates are treated (e.g., a candidate that doesn't terminate at a newline within max_new_tokens).\\n\\n4) Every source-dependent detail (RepoBench-C version, field names, prompt schema, cross_file_first file-ordering) must be stated as an assumption, each with an explicit verification step to run in the real execution environment — nothing is on disk and the sandbox is offline.\\n\\n5) Make the feasibility section concrete: CPU-only inference strategy for CodeLlama-7b (FP16 in ~2 TB RAM, bf16 or 8-bit fallback via bitsandbytes if memory/throughput binds), pinned torch/transformers on Python 3.10/3.11, projected throughput, a time budget with the N=400 / fallback N=200·n=5 seeded (seed 42) subsetting rule, and a contingency if model downloads or license acceptance fail.\\n\\nNow produce the full final experiment design deliverable — rationale, experiment setup (model instantiation, prompt construction, decoding config), controls, analysis plan (precise metrics and statistical reporting), and feasibility — as a single coherent document suitable for submission. No experiments are to be run or claimed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "6bf6c20e-85ce-4807-adb7-5e0c2fca233f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:11:29.518193", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0eddad34-19d3-4803-9084-e74e2e2ee653", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the approach and wants the full final design document. I have all the exploration evidence I need. Let me produce a comprehensive, coherent final experiment design deliverable as stage=result.\n\nLet me structure it well:\n\n1. Rationale / research question\n2. Design-only scope confirmation\n3. Input inventory (what was verified on disk)\n4. Experiment setup:\n   - Models & instantiation\n   - Environment/setup steps (pinned versions)\n   - Data: RepoBench-C Python split assumptions + verification steps\n   - Prompt construction & cross_file_first\n   - Token budget/windowing\n   - Decoding config\n5. Controls (fairness controls, single-variable)\n6. Analysis plan (precise metrics, Pass@k estimator, bootstrap CIs, edge-case handling, secondary analyses)\n7. Feasibility (CPU inference strategy, throughput projection, time budget, subsetting rules, contingencies)\n8. Assumptions table with verification steps\n9. Uncertainties / limitations\n10. Reproducibility (seed, config, artifact record)\n\nLet me make it concrete and submission-ready. This is a design-only task; no experiment run claimed.\n\nLet me write the final answer as a coherent document.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design (Final): GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench-C Python — `cross_file_first`\n\n**Status: design-only.** No experiment was run or claimed. This document is a fully executable experimental plan; the offline sandbox has no datasets, model weights, or ML packages, so all source-dependent details are stated as assumptions, each with an explicit verification step for the real execution environment.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question & rationale\n\n**Q:** How does the performance of a small language model (GPT-2 family) compare to a larger code-specialized model (CodeLlama-7b) on the RepoBench Python dataset for the `cross_file_first` setting?\n\n**Rationale for the design choices:**\n- **RepoBench-C** (Tian et al., 2023) tests repository-level code completion: given related files plus the target file's preceding code, predict the **next line**. `cross_file_first` is the setting where the target's relevant files are placed *before* the target prefix in the prompt.\n- Hitting this with a **single-variable controlled comparison** isolates model capability: identical prompt template, identical token budget, identical decoding. The only systematic difference must be the model.\n- **GPT-2 (124M)** is the canonical small causal LM; **gpt2-xl (1.5B)** gives within-family scaling evidence. **CodeLlama-7b-hf** (base, code-completion variant) is the ~45× larger code-specialized comparator. The GPT-2 vs CodeLlama contrast conflates *scale* and *code specialization*; this is acknowledged and reported as such (the pair alone cannot separate the two, but the gpt2→gpt2-xl arm isolates scale and the CodeLlama full-window arm isolates context capacity).\n- To avoid the dominant confound (GPT-2's 1024-token window vs CodeLlama's 16k), both models are given a **shared, GPT-2-sized context budget** as the *primary* comparison; a labeled **secondary** CodeLlama run at its full 16k window quantifies how much of the gap is pure context capacity.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Input inventory (verified facts, from the offline sandbox)\n\n| Item | Verified state |\n|---|---|\n| Workspace `/workspace` | Contains only `task.json` |\n| `task.json` documents → `track.md` | Text: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"* (file not physically present) |\n| Network | Disabled (DNS resolution fails; `pip download` fails) |\n| GPU | None (`gpu_enabled: false`) |\n| CPU / RAM | 224 logical CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, ~17 GB free disk on mount |\n| Datasets | Provided `datasets: []` — no RepoBench data on disk |\n| Python | 3.14.4 at `/workspace`; `torch`, `transformers`, `datasets`, `tokenizers`, `numpy`, `pandas`, `safetensors`, `accelerate`, `sentencepiece`, `protobuf`, HF caches: **all absent** |\n\n**Consequence:** every dataset/model/prompt-schema detail below comes from the public RepoBench paper/repo and Hub model cards, and is listed in §7 as an assumption with a verification step to run in the execution environment.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Environment & setup (execution environment, not the sandbox)\n\n**Step 3.1 — Verify assumptions first (see §7 checklist), then build:**\n\n1. Create a **Python 3.11 venv** (pinned: `python=3.11`, because `torch` does not build/run cleanly on 3.14; this is an explicit environment decision).\n2. Install pinned packages:\n   - `torch==2.3.1` (CPU build), `transformers==4.44.2`, `datasets==2.21.0`, `accelerate==0.33.0`, `sentencepiece==0.2.0`, `protobuf==4.25.3`, `numpy==1.26.4`, `safetensors==0.4.5`, `bitsandbytes==0.43.3` (for 8-bit fallback), `scipy` (bootstrap not strictly needed — implement bootstrap manually with numpy).\n3. Download and cache the datasset and weights:\n   - Dataset: RepoBench-C Python split from HuggingFace (`leogao2/repobench`; revision pinned at download time).\n   - Weights: `gpt2`, `gpt2-xl`, `meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf` (requires accepting Meta's license + HF token `huggingface-cli login`).\n4. Record exact commit SHAs / dataset revision strings and package versions into a `environment.lock` / `CONFIG.yaml` for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experiment setup\n\n### 4.1 Models & instantiation\n- **GPT-2 / GPT-2-xl:** `transformers.GPT2LMHeadModel.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\"|\"gpt2-xl\")`, `torch_dtype=\"auto\"` (fp32 fine on CPU; ~0.5 GB/1.75 GB weights). EOS/pad: model defaults (`eos_token_id=50256`).\n- **CodeLlama-7b:** `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf\", torch_dtype=torch.float16, low_cpu_mem_usage=True)` — ~13 GB of weights, trivially fits the ~2 TB of RAM. Run in `torch.no_grad()` with `torch.set_num_threads(224)`. **Fallback (only if memory or throughput binds):** `load_in_8bit=True` via `bitsandbytes` (CPU), or `torch.bfloat16`; record which path was taken in `CONFIG.yaml`. `torch_dtype=float16` with `low_cpu_mem_usage=True` is the primary path.\n\n### 4.2 Data: RepoBench-C Python split (assumptions flagged in §7)\n- Each sample supplies: the **target file** (name + full content), the **golden next line** to predict, and the **context/related files** (the files RepoBench marks as relevant to the target).\n- The `cross_file_first` setting = context files appear **before** the target file's prefix in the prompt. File ordering within the prompt = the dataset's documented order (assumption A2).\n- Exact field names (e.g., `context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`) and the dataset revision are **verified at run time** against the downloaded catalog (A1); the loader is written defensively to assert their presence and abort with a clear message if the schema differs.\n\n### 4.3 Prompt construction & windowing (identical for both models in the primary comparison)\nAssembled prompt (single string, same template for every sample and both models):\n\n```\n[context file 1 contents]\n[...context file k contents...]\n# ---- target file: <target_path> ----\n<target file prefix: ALL source text strictly before the golden line>\n```\n\n**Windowing rule (deterministic, exact, shared token budget = 1024):**\n1. Tokenize the target prefix first; **always keep the full target prefix** (it is the most predictive content). If the prefix alone exceeds the budget, keep its **last** 1024–R tokens (suffix rule), where R = generation reserve (see 4.4).\n2. Fill the remaining budget (after the prefix) with context files **in dataset order** (most-relevant first), adding whole files via `add_tokens` accounting; if a file would overflow, **drop that file and all later files** (never truncate mid-file, never drop the prefix).\n3. Apply the *identical* rule to both models so both see the same sequence *modulo their own tokenizers*. Because GPT-2 BPE and CodeLlama SentencePiece tokenize differently, \"1024 tokens\" is not byte-identical across models — this residual is acknowledged (assumption A9) and its size reported as `mean(prompt_tokens_gpt2) − mean(prompt_tokens_codellama)` so the reader can judge it.\n4. No chat template / no instruction prefix is added (both are base completion models; adding one would itself be a confound).\n\n### 4.4 Decoding configuration (fixed; only the model varies)\nPrimary (greedy → Pass@1): `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `pad_token_id` and `eos_token_id` per model, `repetition_penalty=1.0`.\nSecondary (sampling → Pass@k): `do_sample=True`, `temperature=1.0`, `top_p=0.95`, `top_k=0`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `repetition_penalty=1.0`, **n=10 candidates per instance, `torch.manual_seed(seed)` with seeds 0–9 reused identically across models** so the only difference is the model.\n- **R = 60** tokens reserved for generation ⇒ prompt budget = 1024 − 60 = **964 tokens** for prefix+context (used in the 4.3 windowing rule).\n- Generation is **stopped at the first newline that terminates a complete logical statement** where deterministically detectable, otherwise at `max_new_tokens`; the \"raw candidate\" (pre-trim) and the \"trimmed candidate\" (up to first newline) are both saved (see §6 edge-case handling).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls (how the head line stays clean)\n1. **Single-variable design:** same prompt template, same windowing rule, same token budget (964 prompt / 60 gen), same generation hyperparameters, same 10 seeds, same subset, same metric code. Only the model changes.\n2. **Shared-cap fairness control (primary):** both models capped at 1024 tokens, so CodeLlama's larger window cannot inflate its headline score.\n3. **Labeled secondary analysis (does not muddy headline):** one additional CodeLlama run at its full **16k window** (same samples, same seeds). Reported in a clearly separated subsection §6.5 as a context-capacity probe, *not* as part of the primary result.\n4. **Determinism:** subset seed 42, sampling seeds 0–9, pinned library versions, greedy run for Pass@1. All generation configs serialized to `CONFIG.yaml` before runs.\n5. **Metric code shared:** one evaluation module, identical normalization and estimators for both models (no per-model branches except EOS/pad handling, which is recorded).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis plan (precise metrics & statistical reporting)\n\n### 6.1 Primary metric: next-line exact-match Pass@k\nNormalization rule (exact, applied identically to gold and candidate strings):\n1. Replace `\\r\\n` and `\\r` with `\\n`; split on `\\n`.\n2. Take the **first non-empty line** as the normalized candidate; if none, candidate = `\"\"` (empty ⇒ wrong).\n3. Strip leading/trailing whitespace on that line; collapse runs of internal whitespace to a single space; compare to the same treatment of the golden line.\n\nCorrect candidate definition: normalized candidate == normalized gold line **and** candidate terminated at a newline (see edge-case rule below); otherwise it counts as incorrect (never \"correct by truncation\").\n\n**Pass@k estimator (Chen et al., HumanEval, unbiased):** per instance with n=10 i.i.d. samples and c correct:\n`pass@k = 1 − C(n−c, k) / C(n, k)`, where `C(a,b)=0 if a<b`, computed in log-space to avoid overflow.\nReport **Pass@1 and Pass@5** for each model.\n\n### 6.2 Secondary metric: syntax-validity rate\nFraction of candidates for which `ast.parse(target_prefix + candidate_line)` succeeds (both on the first non-empty line and on the full raw candidate — reported separately as strict vs. lenient). Requires a `python` grammar that matches the dataset's (assumption A10; see §7). If a syntax checker is not installable, this metric is dropped and reported as \"not computed,\" not approximated.\n\n### 6.3 Statistical reporting\n- Point estimates for Pass@1/Pass@5 and syntax-validity per model.\n- **Bootstrap 95% CIs** over the *instance* dimension (resample instances, B=10,000, fixed RNG seed 123) for each Pass@k — not over candidates within an instance (that variance is already inside the estimator).\n- Head-to-head **delta** $\\Delta = \\mathrm{Pass@1}_{CodeLlama} − \\mathrm{Pass@1}_{GPT2}$ with its bootstrap CI, plus a paired sign test on per-instance 0/1 greedy correctness.\n- Report the residual window-size mismatch (mean token-count difference per model) and the candidate termination rate per model (so \"CodeLlama generates longer\" noise is visible).\n\n### 6.4 Edge-case handling (exact rules)\n- **Empty candidate** (0 tokens generated): wrong for exact match; `ast.parse(\"\")` counts as syntactically valid only if the target prefix is parseable standalone — report such cases separately (usually a prefix with an open block is not parseable alone).\n- **No newline within `max_new_tokens`:** candidate is a **truncated** raw string ⇒ cannot match (rule in 6.1); counted as wrong for exact match and **excluded** from the syntax-validity numerator by default (reported alongside as \"X% truncated\").\n- **Generation error / empty logits:** counted as empty candidate (wrong); logged.\n- **Duplicate sampled candidates**: kept in n for estimator correctness (estimator already handles c counts); reported as duplicate rate for transparency (sanity check on temperature=1.0 diversity).\n\n### 6.5 Secondary analyses (clearly labeled, not headline)\n- **S1 — Context-capacity probe:** CodeLlama at full 16k window, same seeds; report `Pass@1_diff = full_window − capped` with CI. This estimates how much of the CodeLlama advantage (or lack thereof) is window size.\n- **S2 — Scaling arm:** gpt2-xl (1.5B) at the same 1024-cap, same seeds; places the GPT-2→CodeLlama gap on a scale curve (scale-only effect), acknowledging it still mixes architecture and training data.\n- **S3 (optional, dropped if infeasible):** execution-based \"pass rate\" — run candidate line in an isolated subprocess (`timeout 10`, `python -I`, `resource`-limited, empty env) comparing stdout/exit. **Explicitly exploratory only**: it is confounded by missing imports/utility functions/environment assumptions and by whether the gold file itself runs; it is never used as the claimed result. If it adds no discriminating signal on a 20-sample pilot, it is dropped.\n\n### 6.6 Reporting format\nTables: model × {Pass@1, Pass@5, syntax-validity, termination rate} with bootstrap CIs; then head-to-head deltas; then S1/S2 as separate labeled subsections. No claim that any of this was run.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assumptions table — each with an explicit verification step in the real environment\n\n| # | Assumption (not a confirmed fact here) | Verification step to run in execution env |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | **RepoBench-C Python split version** (e.g., v1.1.x) and exact field names (`context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`), prompt schema | After `datasets.load_dataset(...)`, print `dataset[\"train\"].features` and 3 samples; assert required fields; pin the dataset revision (commit SHA) in `CONFIG.yaml` |\n| A2 | **`cross_file_first` definition**: relevant files placed before the target prefix, in dataset-documented order | Read the dataset card / RepoBench repo instructions at run time; assert that the loaded sample exposes context files and target prefix in the expected fields; if the schema encodes a different ordering, adopt the *documented* order and record it |\n| A3 | **Weights obtainable**: `gpt2`, `gpt2-xl`, `CodeLlama-7b-hf` downloadable after license acceptance | `huggingface-cli login`; attempt `from_pretrained` in a smoke test; record weight file SHAs/`revision` |\n| A4 | **Python 3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers** (sandbox's 3.14 + no packages ≠ execution env) | Create venv; run a 1-minute smoke generation with `gpt2` to confirm install integrity |\n| A5 | **Shared 1024-token cap** is the primary fairness control; full-window CodeLlama is a labeled secondary probe (not headline) | Enforced by shared windowing function; CI checks in test suite |\n| A6 | **Pass@k convention**: n=10, seeds 0–9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator | Unit test estimator against a hand-computed (n=10, c) case |\n| A7 | **Subset rule**: seeded (seed 42) stratified sample of N=400; fallback N=200 with n=5; deterministic | Log subset indices; rerun a 10-instance slice to confirm identical IDs |\n| A8 | **Exact-match normalization** (newline, first-non-empty-line, whitespace-collapse rule) | Unit tests with hand-crafted gold/candidate pairs incl. empty/truncated cases |\n| A9 | **Tokenizer/VLA mismatch** is bounded by the shared cap and reported | Print mean prompt-token difference between models for the subset |\n| A10 | **Syntax-validity** via `ast.parse` matches the dataset's grammar/expected Python version | Check Python version used to author the RepoBench-C split (dataset card); use matching grammar; if mismatch, report syntax-validity as approximate or drop it |\n\n**Contingency rules:** (a) if *any* A1/A2/A3 verification fails (e.g., license refused or dataset revision differs from expected), the design must be re-scoped with the researcher — do **not** silently substitute data; (b) if only the CodeLlama weight download fails, the GPT-2 arm and pipeline still stand as a validated harness (reported as \"CodeLlama arm not run, reason recorded\"); (c) if the dataset is unavailable entirely, no run occurs and nothing is claimed.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility\n\n- **Memory:** CodeLlama-7b fp16 ≈ 13 GB ≪ ~2 TB available; gpt2-xl ≈ 1.75 GB; no GPU required. The primary memory risk is none; 8-bit fallback is available if a different (smaller) machine is used.\n- **Throughput projection (CPU, 224 threads):** CodeLlama-7b fp16 forward ≈ 20–60 total tokens/s on a large multi-socket CPU host (optimistic; 5–10 tok/s is a safe planning figure). Budgeting the safe figure: 400 instances × 10 candidates × 60 generated tokens = 240,000 generated tokens ⇒ ~6.6–13 h on 224 cores for the *sampling* run, plus ~1–2 h for the greedy run with the same prompt encodings; caching prompt KV/computed token ids across candidates (cache the 964-token prompt **once** per instance, generate 10 continuations from the same cache) substantially cuts waste. The **full-window 16k CodeLlama probe** is the most expensive arm (~4× tokens ⇒ ~26–52 h); this is why it is a labeled, optional secondary and may be limited to N=200.\n- **Time budget & subset rules (hard, exact):**\n  - Primary run: N=400, n=10, seeds 0–9, budget ≤ ~24 h wall.\n  - If the CodeLlama sampling arm exceeds 24 h: fallback to **N=200, n=5** (same seed 42 subset is still deterministic and comparable), recorded in `CONFIG.yaml`.\n  - If it still overruns at N=200/n=5 (or memory pressure appears), run CodeLlama in 8-bit and/or reduce `max_new_tokens` to 40 (recorded); do not change the metric definition.\n- **Disk:** ~15–20 GB needed for weights + dataset + caches; the sandbox mount had only 17 GB free, so the execution environment must ensure ≥ 40 GB free (documented requirement).\n- **Feasibility checks before the full run** (small, cheap): (i) 5-instance smoke test of the full pipeline on `gpt2` (assert no crashes, correct field access); (ii) 20-instance pilot with `gpt2` and `gpt2-xl` to validate metrics and CI code; (iii) single CodeLlama batch-timed forward on 3 instances to calibrate the throughput estimate used above, triggering the fallback subset rule if needed.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Reproducibility & deliverable structure\n\n- `CONFIG.yaml`: dataset revision/SHA, model revisions, seeds (subset=42, sampling=0–9, bootstrap=123), windowing constants (1024/964/60), decoding args, subset rule, dtype/fallback path chosen.\n- `environment.lock`: pinned package versions + Python version.\n- Saved artifacts per model: `{model}_greedy.jsonl` and `{model}_sample.jsonl` (instance_id, prompt token ids, all 10 raw + trimmed candidates, per-candidate termination flag, gold line), so every reported number is reconstructible from raw outputs.\n- Single `README.md` documenting A1–A10, chosen vs. rejected options, and that this is a **design/executed-by-others plan** — no experiment was run in this task.\n\n## 10. Limitations (stated, not hidden)\n- GPT-2 vs CodeLlama conflates scale and code-specialization; the gpt2-xl arm (S2) and CodeLlama full-window arm (S1) partly decompose this but are not confound-free.\n- All RepoBench-C schema/definition details are memory-based until verified in the execution environment (§7).\n- The execution-based pass-rate idea (S3) is confounded by imports/environment and is exploratory or dropped; the claimed primary results are **next-line exact-match Pass@k** and **syntax-validity rate** only.\n- CPU-only inference bounds the subset size (N=400, fallback N=200·n=5), so results are point estimates on a seeded subset with bootstrap CIs, not full-test-set claims.", "evidence": "Verified on disk in the offline sandbox: /workspace contains only task.json; task.json documents.track.md reads \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" — no track.md file physically present, no datasets, no model weights, no eval scripts. Runtime confirmed: network disabled (DNSc resolution fails; pip download fails), gpu_enabled false, 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, datasets [] . Python 3.14.4 with importlib checks showing torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy/safetensors/accelerate/huggingface_hub/sentencepiece/protobuf all absent; no HF cache dirs. Design therefore pins every external detail as an assumption (A1-A10) each with a run-time verification step, and includes contingency rules for failed verification/downloads.", "assumptions": ["A1: RepoBench-C Python split revision and field schema (to be verified via dataset features dump, pinned in config).", "A2: cross_file_first = relevant files before target prefix in documented order (verified from dataset card/repo at run time).", "A3: gpt2/gpt2-xl and CodeLlama-7b-hf weights obtainable after license acceptance in the execution environment.", "A4: implementation runs on Python 3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers; sandbox Python 3.14 is not the execution env.", "A5: shared 1024-token cap (964 prompt / 60 gen) is the primary fairness control; 16k CodeLlama run is a labeled secondary probe only.", "A6: Pass@k uses n=10, seeds 0-9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator (unit-tested).", "A7: subset = seed-42 stratified N=400, fallback N=200 with n=5, deterministic and logged.", "A8: exact-match normalization = newline split, first non-empty line, collapse whitespace; truncated candidates always wrong.", "A9: tokenizer/VLA mismatch bounded by shared cap; residual mean-token-difference reported.", "A10: ast.parse-based syntax validity assumes a Python grammar matching the dataset authoring version; else dropped or labeled approximate.", "S3 execution-based pass rate is exploratory only and dropped if the pilot shows no signal, due to imports/environment confounds."], "uncertainties": ["Exact RepoBench-C field names, dataset revision, and cross_file_first ordering not verifiable offline (A1/A2) — must be confirmed in the execution environment before any run.", "Actual CPU throughput of CodeLlama-7b on the execution host (plan uses a safe 5-10 tok/s figure and activates a N=200/n=5 fallback if the 24h budget is exceeded).", "Whether ast.parse-based syntax validity matches the dataset's authoring Python version (A10).", "Whether execution-level 'pass rate' (S3) is meaningful at all — marked exploratory/droppable. Claimed primary result is exact-match Pass@k + syntax-validity rate."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b1e958978d9a475a9a49a46f2c47d535", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b1e958978d9a475a9a49a46f2c47d535", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Experiment Design (Final): GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench-C Python \\u2014 `cross_file_first`\\n\\n**Status: design-only.** No experiment was run or claimed. This document is a fully executable experimental plan; the offline sandbox has no datasets, model weights, or ML packages, so all source-dependent details are stated as assumptions, each with an explicit verification step for the real execution environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research question & rationale\\n\\n**Q:** How does the performance of a small language model (GPT-2 family) compare to a larger code-specialized model (CodeLlama-7b) on the RepoBench Python dataset for the `cross_file_first` setting?\\n\\n**Rationale for the design choices:**\\n- **RepoBench-C** (Tian et al., 2023) tests repository-level code completion: given related files plus the target file's preceding code, predict the **next line**. `cross_file_first` is the setting where the target's relevant files are placed *before* the target prefix in the prompt.\\n- Hitting this with a **single-variable controlled comparison** isolates model capability: identical prompt template, identical token budget, identical decoding. The only systematic difference must be the model.\\n- **GPT-2 (124M)** is the canonical small causal LM; **gpt2-xl (1.5B)** gives within-family scaling evidence. **CodeLlama-7b-hf** (base, code-completion variant) is the ~45\\u00d7 larger code-specialized comparator. The GPT-2 vs CodeLlama contrast conflates *scale* and *code specialization*; this is acknowledged and reported as such (the pair alone cannot separate the two, but the gpt2\\u2192gpt2-xl arm isolates scale and the CodeLlama full-window arm isolates context capacity).\\n- To avoid the dominant confound (GPT-2's 1024-token window vs CodeLlama's 16k), both models are given a **shared, GPT-2-sized context budget** as the *primary* comparison; a labeled **secondary** CodeLlama run at its full 16k window quantifies how much of the gap is pure context capacity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Input inventory (verified facts, from the offline sandbox)\\n\\n| Item | Verified state |\\n|---|---|\\n| Workspace `/workspace` | Contains only `task.json` |\\n| `task.json` documents \\u2192 `track.md` | Text: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"* (file not physically present) |\\n| Network | Disabled (DNS resolution fails; `pip download` fails) |\\n| GPU | None (`gpu_enabled: false`) |\\n| CPU / RAM | 224 logical CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, ~17 GB free disk on mount |\\n| Datasets | Provided `datasets: []` \\u2014 no RepoBench data on disk |\\n| Python | 3.14.4 at `/workspace`; `torch`, `transformers`, `datasets`, `tokenizers`, `numpy`, `pandas`, `safetensors`, `accelerate`, `sentencepiece`, `protobuf`, HF caches: **all absent** |\\n\\n**Consequence:** every dataset/model/prompt-schema detail below comes from the public RepoBench paper/repo and Hub model cards, and is listed in \\u00a77 as an assumption with a verification step to run in the execution environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Environment & setup (execution environment, not the sandbox)\\n\\n**Step 3.1 \\u2014 Verify assumptions first (see \\u00a77 checklist), then build:**\\n\\n1. Create a **Python 3.11 venv** (pinned: `python=3.11`, because `torch` does not build/run cleanly on 3.14; this is an explicit environment decision).\\n2. Install pinned packages:\\n   - `torch==2.3.1` (CPU build), `transformers==4.44.2`, `datasets==2.21.0`, `accelerate==0.33.0`, `sentencepiece==0.2.0`, `protobuf==4.25.3`, `numpy==1.26.4`, `safetensors==0.4.5`, `bitsandbytes==0.43.3` (for 8-bit fallback), `scipy` (bootstrap not strictly needed \\u2014 implement bootstrap manually with numpy).\\n3. Download and cache the datasset and weights:\\n   - Dataset: RepoBench-C Python split from HuggingFace (`leogao2/repobench`; revision pinned at download time).\\n   - Weights: `gpt2`, `gpt2-xl`, `meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf` (requires accepting Meta's license + HF token `huggingface-cli login`).\\n4. Record exact commit SHAs / dataset revision strings and package versions into a `environment.lock` / `CONFIG.yaml` for reproducibility.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Experiment setup\\n\\n### 4.1 Models & instantiation\\n- **GPT-2 / GPT-2-xl:** `transformers.GPT2LMHeadModel.from_pretrained(\\\"gpt2\\\"|\\\"gpt2-xl\\\")`, `torch_dtype=\\\"auto\\\"` (fp32 fine on CPU; ~0.5 GB/1.75 GB weights). EOS/pad: model defaults (`eos_token_id=50256`).\\n- **CodeLlama-7b:** `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\\\"meta-llama/CodeLlama-7b-hf\\\", torch_dtype=torch.float16, low_cpu_mem_usage=True)` \\u2014 ~13 GB of weights, trivially fits the ~2 TB of RAM. Run in `torch.no_grad()` with `torch.set_num_threads(224)`. **Fallback (only if memory or throughput binds):** `load_in_8bit=True` via `bitsandbytes` (CPU), or `torch.bfloat16`; record which path was taken in `CONFIG.yaml`. `torch_dtype=float16` with `low_cpu_mem_usage=True` is the primary path.\\n\\n### 4.2 Data: RepoBench-C Python split (assumptions flagged in \\u00a77)\\n- Each sample supplies: the **target file** (name + full content), the **golden next line** to predict, and the **context/related files** (the files RepoBench marks as relevant to the target).\\n- The `cross_file_first` setting = context files appear **before** the target file's prefix in the prompt. File ordering within the prompt = the dataset's documented order (assumption A2).\\n- Exact field names (e.g., `context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`) and the dataset revision are **verified at run time** against the downloaded catalog (A1); the loader is written defensively to assert their presence and abort with a clear message if the schema differs.\\n\\n### 4.3 Prompt construction & windowing (identical for both models in the primary comparison)\\nAssembled prompt (single string, same template for every sample and both models):\\n\\n```\\n[context file 1 contents]\\n[...context file k contents...]\\n# ---- target file: <target_path> ----\\n<target file prefix: ALL source text strictly before the golden line>\\n```\\n\\n**Windowing rule (deterministic, exact, shared token budget = 1024):**\\n1. Tokenize the target prefix first; **always keep the full target prefix** (it is the most predictive content). If the prefix alone exceeds the budget, keep its **last** 1024\\u2013R tokens (suffix rule), where R = generation reserve (see 4.4).\\n2. Fill the remaining budget (after the prefix) with context files **in dataset order** (most-relevant first), adding whole files via `add_tokens` accounting; if a file would overflow, **drop that file and all later files** (never truncate mid-file, never drop the prefix).\\n3. Apply the *identical* rule to both models so both see the same sequence *modulo their own tokenizers*. Because GPT-2 BPE and CodeLlama SentencePiece tokenize differently, \\\"1024 tokens\\\" is not byte-identical across models \\u2014 this residual is acknowledged (assumption A9) and its size reported as `mean(prompt_tokens_gpt2) \\u2212 mean(prompt_tokens_codellama)` so the reader can judge it.\\n4. No chat template / no instruction prefix is added (both are base completion models; adding one would itself be a confound).\\n\\n### 4.4 Decoding configuration (fixed; only the model varies)\\nPrimary (greedy \\u2192 Pass@1): `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `pad_token_id` and `eos_token_id` per model, `repetition_penalty=1.0`.\\nSecondary (sampling \\u2192 Pass@k): `do_sample=True`, `temperature=1.0`, `top_p=0.95`, `top_k=0`, `max_new_tokens=60`, `repetition_penalty=1.0`, **n=10 candidates per instance, `torch.manual_seed(seed)` with seeds 0\\u20139 reused identically across models** so the only difference is the model.\\n- **R = 60** tokens reserved for generation \\u21d2 prompt budget = 1024 \\u2212 60 = **964 tokens** for prefix+context (used in the 4.3 windowing rule).\\n- Generation is **stopped at the first newline that terminates a complete logical statement** where deterministically detectable, otherwise at `max_new_tokens`; the \\\"raw candidate\\\" (pre-trim) and the \\\"trimmed candidate\\\" (up to first newline) are both saved (see \\u00a76 edge-case handling).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Controls (how the head line stays clean)\\n1. **Single-variable design:** same prompt template, same windowing rule, same token budget (964 prompt / 60 gen), same generation hyperparameters, same 10 seeds, same subset, same metric code. Only the model changes.\\n2. **Shared-cap fairness control (primary):** both models capped at 1024 tokens, so CodeLlama's larger window cannot inflate its headline score.\\n3. **Labeled secondary analysis (does not muddy headline):** one additional CodeLlama run at its full **16k window** (same samples, same seeds). Reported in a clearly separated subsection \\u00a76.5 as a context-capacity probe, *not* as part of the primary result.\\n4. **Determinism:** subset seed 42, sampling seeds 0\\u20139, pinned library versions, greedy run for Pass@1. All generation configs serialized to `CONFIG.yaml` before runs.\\n5. **Metric code shared:** one evaluation module, identical normalization and estimators for both models (no per-model branches except EOS/pad handling, which is recorded).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan (precise metrics & statistical reporting)\\n\\n### 6.1 Primary metric: next-line exact-match Pass@k\\nNormalization rule (exact, applied identically to gold and candidate strings):\\n1. Replace `\\\\r\\\\n` and `\\\\r` with `\\\\n`; split on `\\\\n`.\\n2. Take the **first non-empty line** as the normalized candidate; if none, candidate = `\\\"\\\"` (empty \\u21d2 wrong).\\n3. Strip leading/trailing whitespace on that line; collapse runs of internal whitespace to a single space; compare to the same treatment of the golden line.\\n\\nCorrect candidate definition: normalized candidate == normalized gold line **and** candidate terminated at a newline (see edge-case rule below); otherwise it counts as incorrect (never \\\"correct by truncation\\\").\\n\\n**Pass@k estimator (Chen et al., HumanEval, unbiased):** per instance with n=10 i.i.d. samples and c correct:\\n`pass@k = 1 \\u2212 C(n\\u2212c, k) / C(n, k)`, where `C(a,b)=0 if a<b`, computed in log-space to avoid overflow.\\nReport **Pass@1 and Pass@5** for each model.\\n\\n### 6.2 Secondary metric: syntax-validity rate\\nFraction of candidates for which `ast.parse(target_prefix + candidate_line)` succeeds (both on the first non-empty line and on the full raw candidate \\u2014 reported separately as strict vs. lenient). Requires a `python` grammar that matches the dataset's (assumption A10; see \\u00a77). If a syntax checker is not installable, this metric is dropped and reported as \\\"not computed,\\\" not approximated.\\n\\n### 6.3 Statistical reporting\\n- Point estimates for Pass@1/Pass@5 and syntax-validity per model.\\n- **Bootstrap 95% CIs** over the *instance* dimension (resample instances, B=10,000, fixed RNG seed 123) for each Pass@k \\u2014 not over candidates within an instance (that variance is already inside the estimator).\\n- Head-to-head **delta** $\\\\Delta = \\\\mathrm{Pass@1}_{CodeLlama} \\u2212 \\\\mathrm{Pass@1}_{GPT2}$ with its bootstrap CI, plus a paired sign test on per-instance 0/1 greedy correctness.\\n- Report the residual window-size mismatch (mean token-count difference per model) and the candidate termination rate per model (so \\\"CodeLlama generates longer\\\" noise is visible).\\n\\n### 6.4 Edge-case handling (exact rules)\\n- **Empty candidate** (0 tokens generated): wrong for exact match; `ast.parse(\\\"\\\")` counts as syntactically valid only if the target prefix is parseable standalone \\u2014 report such cases separately (usually a prefix with an open block is not parseable alone).\\n- **No newline within `max_new_tokens`:** candidate is a **truncated** raw string \\u21d2 cannot match (rule in 6.1); counted as wrong for exact match and **excluded** from the syntax-validity numerator by default (reported alongside as \\\"X% truncated\\\").\\n- **Generation error / empty logits:** counted as empty candidate (wrong); logged.\\n- **Duplicate sampled candidates**: kept in n for estimator correctness (estimator already handles c counts); reported as duplicate rate for transparency (sanity check on temperature=1.0 diversity).\\n\\n### 6.5 Secondary analyses (clearly labeled, not headline)\\n- **S1 \\u2014 Context-capacity probe:** CodeLlama at full 16k window, same seeds; report `Pass@1_diff = full_window \\u2212 capped` with CI. This estimates how much of the CodeLlama advantage (or lack thereof) is window size.\\n- **S2 \\u2014 Scaling arm:** gpt2-xl (1.5B) at the same 1024-cap, same seeds; places the GPT-2\\u2192CodeLlama gap on a scale curve (scale-only effect), acknowledging it still mixes architecture and training data.\\n- **S3 (optional, dropped if infeasible):** execution-based \\\"pass rate\\\" \\u2014 run candidate line in an isolated subprocess (`timeout 10`, `python -I`, `resource`-limited, empty env) comparing stdout/exit. **Explicitly exploratory only**: it is confounded by missing imports/utility functions/environment assumptions and by whether the gold file itself runs; it is never used as the claimed result. If it adds no discriminating signal on a 20-sample pilot, it is dropped.\\n\\n### 6.6 Reporting format\\nTables: model \\u00d7 {Pass@1, Pass@5, syntax-validity, termination rate} with bootstrap CIs; then head-to-head deltas; then S1/S2 as separate labeled subsections. No claim that any of this was run.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Assumptions table \\u2014 each with an explicit verification step in the real environment\\n\\n| # | Assumption (not a confirmed fact here) | Verification step to run in execution env |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | **RepoBench-C Python split version** (e.g., v1.1.x) and exact field names (`context_files`, `next_line`/`golden_line`), prompt schema | After `datasets.load_dataset(...)`, print `dataset[\\\"train\\\"].features` and 3 samples; assert required fields; pin the dataset revision (commit SHA) in `CONFIG.yaml` |\\n| A2 | **`cross_file_first` definition**: relevant files placed before the target prefix, in dataset-documented order | Read the dataset card / RepoBench repo instructions at run time; assert that the loaded sample exposes context files and target prefix in the expected fields; if the schema encodes a different ordering, adopt the *documented* order and record it |\\n| A3 | **Weights obtainable**: `gpt2`, `gpt2-xl`, `CodeLlama-7b-hf` downloadable after license acceptance | `huggingface-cli login`; attempt `from_pretrained` in a smoke test; record weight file SHAs/`revision` |\\n| A4 | **Python 3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers** (sandbox's 3.14 + no packages \\u2260 execution env) | Create venv; run a 1-minute smoke generation with `gpt2` to confirm install integrity |\\n| A5 | **Shared 1024-token cap** is the primary fairness control; full-window CodeLlama is a labeled secondary probe (not headline) | Enforced by shared windowing function; CI checks in test suite |\\n| A6 | **Pass@k convention**: n=10, seeds 0\\u20139, Chen et al. unbiased estimator | Unit test estimator against a hand-computed (n=10, c) case |\\n| A7 | **Subset rule**: seeded (seed 42) stratified sample of N=400; fallback N=200 with n=5; deterministic | Log subset indices; rerun a 10-instance slice to confirm identical IDs |\\n| A8 | **Exact-match normalization** (newline, first-non-empty-line, whitespace-collapse rule) | Unit tests with hand-crafted gold/candidate pairs incl. empty/truncated cases |\\n| A9 | **Tokenizer/VLA mismatch** is bounded by the shared cap and reported | Print mean prompt-token difference between models for the subset |\\n| A10 | **Syntax-validity** via `ast.parse` matches the dataset's grammar/expected Python version | Check Python version used to author the RepoBench-C split (dataset card); use matching grammar; if mismatch, report syntax-validity as approximate or drop it |\\n\\n**Contingency rules:** (a) if *any* A1/A2/A3 verification fails (e.g., license refused or dataset revision differs from expected), the design must be re-scoped with the researcher \\u2014 do **not** silently substitute data; (b) if only the CodeLlama weight download fails, the GPT-2 arm and pipeline still stand as a validated harness (reported as \\\"CodeLlama arm not run, reason recorded\\\"); (c) if the dataset is unavailable entirely, no run occurs and nothing is claimed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility\\n\\n- **Memory:** CodeLlama-7b fp16 \\u2248 13 GB \\u226a ~2 TB available; gpt2-xl \\u2248 1.75 GB; no GPU required. The primary memory risk is none; 8-bit fallback is available if a different (smaller) machine is used.\\n- **Throughput projection (CPU, 224 threads):** CodeLlama-7b fp16 forward \\u2248 20\\u201360 total tokens/s on a large multi-socket CPU host (optimistic; 5\\u201310 tok/s is a safe planning figure). Budgeting the safe figure: 400 instances \\u00d7 10 candidates \\u00d7 60 generated tokens = 240,000 generated tokens \\u21d2 ~6.6\\u201313 h on 224 cores for the *sampling* run, plus ~1\\u20132 h for the greedy run with the same prompt encodings; caching prompt KV/computed token ids across candidates (cache the 964-token prompt **once** per instance, generate 10 continuations from the same cache) substantially cuts waste. The **full-window 16k CodeLlama probe** is the most expensive arm (~4\\u00d7 tokens \\u21d2 ~26\\u201352 h); this is why it is a labeled, optional secondary and may be limited to N=200.\\n- **Time budget & subset rules (hard, exact):**\\n  - Primary run: N=400, n=10, seeds 0\\u20139, budget \\u2264 ~24 h wall.\\n  - If the CodeLlama sampling arm exceeds 24 h: fallback to **N=200, n=5** (same seed 42 subset is still deterministic and comparable), recorded in `CONFIG.yaml`.\\n  - If it still overruns at N=200/n=5 (or memory pressure appears), run CodeLlama in 8-bit and/or reduce `max_new_tokens` to 40 (recorded); do not change the metric definition.\\n- **Disk:** ~15\\u201320 GB needed for weights + dataset + caches; the sandbox mount had only 17 GB free, so the execution environment must ensure \\u2265 40 GB free (documented requirement).\\n- **Feasibility checks before the full run** (small, cheap): (i) 5-instance smoke test of the full pipeline on `gpt2` (assert no crashes, correct field access); (ii) 20-instance pilot with `gpt2` and `gpt2-xl` to validate metrics and CI code; (iii) single CodeLlama batch-timed forward on 3 instances to calibrate the throughput estimate used above, triggering the fallback subset rule if needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Reproducibility & deliverable structure\\n\\n- `CONFIG.yaml`: dataset revision/SHA, model revisions, seeds (subset=42, sampling=0\\u20139, bootstrap=123), windowing constants (1024/964/60), decoding args, subset rule, dtype/fallback path chosen.\\n- `environment.lock`: pinned package versions + Python version.\\n- Saved artifacts per model: `{model}_greedy.jsonl` and `{model}_sample.jsonl` (instance_id, prompt token ids, all 10 raw + trimmed candidates, per-candidate termination flag, gold line), so every reported number is reconstructible from raw outputs.\\n- Single `README.md` documenting A1\\u2013A10, chosen vs. rejected options, and that this is a **design/executed-by-others plan** \\u2014 no experiment was run in this task.\\n\\n## 10. Limitations (stated, not hidden)\\n- GPT-2 vs CodeLlama conflates scale and code-specialization; the gpt2-xl arm (S2) and CodeLlama full-window arm (S1) partly decompose this but are not confound-free.\\n- All RepoBench-C schema/definition details are memory-based until verified in the execution environment (\\u00a77).\\n- The execution-based pass-rate idea (S3) is confounded by imports/environment and is exploratory or dropped; the claimed primary results are **next-line exact-match Pass@k** and **syntax-validity rate** only.\\n- CPU-only inference bounds the subset size (N=400, fallback N=200\\u00b7n=5), so results are point estimates on a seeded subset with bootstrap CIs, not full-test-set claims.\", \"evidence\": \"Verified on disk in the offline sandbox: /workspace contains only task.json; task.json documents.track.md reads \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" \\u2014 no track.md file physically present, no datasets, no model weights, no eval scripts. Runtime confirmed: network disabled (DNSc resolution fails; pip download fails), gpu_enabled false, 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, datasets [] . Python 3.14.4 with importlib checks showing torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy/safetensors/accelerate/huggingface_hub/sentencepiece/protobuf all absent; no HF cache dirs. Design therefore pins every external detail as an assumption (A1-A10) each with a run-time verification step, and includes contingency rules for failed verification/downloads.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: RepoBench-C Python split revision and field schema (to be verified via dataset features dump, pinned in config).\", \"A2: cross_file_first = relevant files before target prefix in documented order (verified from dataset card/repo at run time).\", \"A3: gpt2/gpt2-xl and CodeLlama-7b-hf weights obtainable after license acceptance in the execution environment.\", \"A4: implementation runs on Python 3.11 venv with pinned torch/transformers; sandbox Python 3.14 is not the execution env.\", \"A5: shared 1024-token cap (964 prompt / 60 gen) is the primary fairness control; 16k CodeLlama run is a labeled secondary probe only.\", \"A6: Pass@k uses n=10, seeds 0-9, Chen et al. unbiased estimator (unit-tested).\", \"A7: subset = seed-42 stratified N=400, fallback N=200 with n=5, deterministic and logged.\", \"A8: exact-match normalization = newline split, first non-empty line, collapse whitespace; truncated candidates always wrong.\", \"A9: tokenizer/VLA mismatch bounded by shared cap; residual mean-token-difference reported.\", \"A10: ast.parse-based syntax validity assumes a Python grammar matching the dataset authoring version; else dropped or labeled approximate.\", \"S3 execution-based pass rate is exploratory only and dropped if the pilot shows no signal, due to imports/environment confounds.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact RepoBench-C field names, dataset revision, and cross_file_first ordering not verifiable offline (A1/A2) \\u2014 must be confirmed in the execution environment before any run.\", \"Actual CPU throughput of CodeLlama-7b on the execution host (plan uses a safe 5-10 tok/s figure and activates a N=200/n=5 fallback if the 24h budget is exceeded).\", \"Whether ast.parse-based syntax validity matches the dataset's authoring Python version (A10).\", \"Whether execution-level 'pass rate' (S3) is meaningful at all \\u2014 marked exploratory/droppable. Claimed primary result is exact-match Pass@k + syntax-validity rate.\"], \"summary\": \"Hand off complete final experiment design for GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench-C cross_file_first\", \"stage\": \"result\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1fe6d390edb04a3bbaec29ae600197de", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Hand off complete final experiment design for GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench-C cross_file_first", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
